The Department of Peace and Conflict Research was established in 1971 to conduct peace research and offer courses in peace and conflict studies. Currently approx. 90 persons are employed at the Department and approximately 1000 students are enrolled every academic year. Courses offered include undergraduate and post-graduate courses, as well as a Ph. D. program. For more information, visit www.pcr.uu.se.

**The project**  
The project *Cooperation across Enemy Lines: Explaining Prisoner Releases and Exchanges in War* examines why warring parties release and exchange prisoners in war, an understudied form of cooperation that is often politically unpopular and strategically costly but surprisingly common. The project will contribute new knowledge on the conditions under which states and armed groups cooperate concerning prisoners in war, focusing on factors such as popular support, conflict dynamics, and international humanitarian law (IHL). The project will build on both interviews with prisoner release mediators and quantitative data, and as part of the project, we will develop the *Prisoners in War Exchange and Release (POWER)* dataset, which covers all armed conflicts in the world between 1989 and 2025. The project is led by project leader Sebastian van Baalen.

